[h1]Changelog[/h1]

[h2]5.0 — Operatives[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Espionage is now genuinely secret.[/b] Operations no longer create diplomatic pacts and no cost modifiers betray your budget: nothing appears in any diplomacy panel, spending is drawn straight from the treasury, and a target learns it is being worked on only when your people are caught.
[*][b]NEW: Operatives.[/b] Recruit named agents with tradecraft specialties - cryptographer, saboteur, master of disguise, silver tongue. Every operation asks who goes and cannot run without someone free; the right specialist tilts the odds. Time on assignment builds experience through Seasoned, Veteran and Master Spy: better odds, better escape chances, and unbreakable silence under interrogation at the top of the ladder.
[*][b]NEW: The Intelligence Console.[/b] A full command panel inside the Shadow War journal entry: your roster with portraits, live status and wages; a Director of Intelligence promoted from the ranks (fire one and their interest group seethes - and one in four sells everything to a hostile power); every running operation with its target's flag, its own monthly cost and its agent's portrait; the total agency budget at the top; and the ballot and manifest sections below.
[*][b]NEW: Spies on the government payroll.[/b] Wages scale with your economy, each agent's experience, and the government wage level you set. Pay Low and idle operatives risk defecting each year - twice as likely at Very Low. The buyer decides their fate: pay for the dossier and every operation against them is rolled up at once, pay extra to poach the agent onto their own roster, or turn the traitor away - and hope the original employer's watchers were asleep, because half the time the treachery is caught coming home.
[*][b]NEW: Counterespionage coups.[/b] Catch a foreign agent in the act and you can move on them, with odds set by the espionage institution edge. Succeed and choose: leave them in place as a turned channel, parade them before the world for scandal and infamy, or lock them in a quiet cell. Fail and they bolt - though the blown network burns either way.
[*][b]NEW: Prisoners and ransoms.[/b] A captured operative sits in foreign cells for up to five years, each year risking that they talk and a network is rolled up while the captor profits. Ransom them home with the new Operative Repatriation treaty article (their captor will want paying), disavow them, or trust their silence - masters never break.
[*][b]Election interference, remade.[/b] Pick any party on the target's ballot from a console section - no more cramped events - and your money floods its campaign with momentum every month, invisibly. One choice per campaign, then results on your desk: exposure craters their electoral confidence and closes the channel, victory seats a well-disposed government that resists your leverage poorly, defeat gets a dry line in the ledger.
[*][b]The sealed train has a manifest.[/b] Smuggle Revolutionary asks who rides: real exiles from the pool, or firebrands of the target's own culture exiled to order, chosen by portrait, ideology and interest group. The sponsor hears the result - the spark catching, or an arrest at the border. Routes burn per country, not globally.
[*][b]Black market slavery flows home.[/b] Fund Black Market Slavery now boosts YOUR slave imports - you are the buyer, the target is the route - and requires your own slavery laws.
[*][b]The pipeline reports its victories.[/b] The Clandestine Arms Pipeline tells you when convoys get through, not only when they are intercepted.
[*][b]The false flag is visible.[/b] The directive confirms itself when laid, shows in the console with its patsy and maintaining agent, carries real upkeep, and can be cancelled early.
[*][b]Kompromat can wait.[/b] A compiled dossier can be filed away unused.
[*][b]NEW: Three new External Security laws.[/b] Consular Intelligence for the respectable early game, the Directorate of State Security for inward-facing regimes, and Deniable Assets for those who prefer their espionage contracted out. The espionage institution now lists which operations each investment level unlocks.
[*]Operation upkeep genuinely scales with the target's economy - richer targets cost proportionally more, and a serious espionage program is a real budget line.
[*]Steal Ship Designs has been removed. The Shadow War journal entry (formerly The Great Game) is pinned by default.
[*][b]This release is not save-compatible.[/b] Please start a new campaign.
[/list]

[h2]4.1 — Shadow War[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]NEW: Clandestine Arms Pipeline[/b] — covertly fund and arm a belligerent without joining their war: real war modifiers for them, a blockade-running interception gauntlet for you, an ultimatum when the enemy finally names you, and an obligation from the survivor when peace comes.
[*][b]NEW: Turned Networks[/b] — victims with a superior espionage institution can secretly turn sow-discontent, election-interference and technology-theft networks: the spymaster keeps paying while receiving nothing — or deliberately poisoned technology. Counterintelligence reviews (skill-weighted) eventually expose the deception.
[*][b]NEW: The Great Game[/b] — a global intelligence scoreboard. Every successful operation scores, scores decay yearly, and the leading service holds [i]Master of the Great Game[/i] with prestige and leverage to match.
[*][b]NEW: The Sealed Train[/b] — smuggle a professional revolutionary into a rival power: a massive radicalism surge with a chance of open revolt, or a border arrest that rallies their loyalists and stains your name.
[*][b]NEW: False Flag Directive[/b] — choose a patsy and your detected operations are blamed on them instead; if the forgery ever unravels, both powers turn on you.
[*][b]NEW: Kompromat[/b] — active spy networks compile yearly dossiers on enemy commanders: disgrace them for a year, end their career outright, or publish selectively to humiliate their government.
[*]Steal Ship Designs has been removed.
[*][b]NEW: Fund Black Market Slavery[/b] — covertly bankroll the contraband slave trade supplying a slaver power. Hugely increases their slave imports, at mounting cost and the risk of exposure before every abolitionist navy. Can be disabled via game rule.
[*][b]NEW: Dynamic odds[/b] — every operation's monthly success odds now scale with the espionage institution differential. Investment is finally tradecraft.
[*]Fixed a bug as old as the mod: running espionage against several countries at once could fire events against the wrong target. Every operation now tracks its own target.
[*]Fund Piracy has been removed — superseded by the base game's naval piracy mechanics and the new espionage systems.
[/list]

[h2]4.0 — Completion & The Great Wave[/h2]
[list]
[*]NEW: Naval Intelligence Network — infiltrate an admiralty through a journal-entry operation balancing Infiltration against Exposure, then choose your coup: crack their codes, cripple their flagship (The Great Wave), or copy their coastal surveys.
[*]NEW: Steal Ship Designs — construction-efficiency and tech-spread espionage against superior navies.
[*]NEW: Blackmailed Concession treaty article — an intelligence dossier compels the target's cooperation; the article collapses if your spy network is rolled up.
[*]Steal Commander completed — true defection via character transfer, with an asset-in-place alternative and a five-year cooldown.
[*]Sabotage Recruitment completed — espionage gate, full localization, conscript unrest.
[*]Develop Nation completed — recognition plus relations, an obligation, and gratitude and prestige rewards.
[*]Fund Piracy reworked onto real 1.13 effects; with The Great Wave, arm the corsairs and take a cut of their plunder.
[*]Sabotage Industry removed (redundant with 1.13 naval mechanics).
[*]Full French localisation for every feature; extensive engine-modernisation fixes (pact auto-cancel logic, character role triggers, AI weights, treaty war-goal enforcement) — see the full notes on GitHub.
[/list]

